-    "findings": "<p>The authors examined nanoplastic-sized material from a polypropylene takeaway box and composite heat-sealing packaging films under hot-water scenarios.</p><p>The polypropylene box and composite films were characterized separately. No particle count or concentration from this study is included in the library. The heat-sealing films are not consumer zip-top storage bags.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>The authors examined nanoplastic-sized material from a polypropylene takeaway box and composite heat-sealing packaging films under hot-water scenarios.</p><p>The polypropylene box and composite films were characterized separately. No particle count or concentration from this study is included in the library. The heat-sealing films are not consumer zip-top storage bags.</p><p>The packaging materials underwent extensive cleaning and chemical treatment before the simulated contact experiments. Water without packaging contact was used for corresponding controls. The materials represented different scenarios, including a takeaway box and films used for composite food packaging. Those treatments and product forms are part of the experiment. They do not describe an untreated package in every household use, and the heat-sealing films should not be relabeled as consumer zip-top bags. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics11070550\">Source: PDF pp. 3-4, sample preparation and Figure 2</a>.</p><p>The workflow separated material with sequential membranes and then prepared specimens for microscopy and dispersed samples for further analysis. Ultrasound was applied during preparation, and the text acknowledges that it can alter particle size and dispersibility. A nominal membrane fraction is therefore not a guarantee that the later dispersion contains only separate objects within that interval. This record retains the characterization context without deriving a release concentration or treating non-detection in another material as general safety. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/toxics11070550\">Source: PDF p. 3 and continuation on p. 4, filtration and ultrasound preparation</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Sequential filtration, electron microscopy, surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy, and laser particle sizing characterized fractions after intensive sample preparation. Water controls were examined. Aggregation and subsequent ultrasound affect interpretation of particle size.",
     "fitness": "Supports qualitative material characterization under the studied scenarios. It supplies no accepted release concentration for this page and does not represent consumer zip-top bags.",
     "limitations": "PP box: 500 mL water at 100 °C for 20 min. GCPP/MCPP composite packaging: 121 °C for 20 min. Intensive detergent, acid, base and prolonged rinse pretreatment differs from ordinary consumer handling. Nominal filter fractions do not mean all dispersed objects stay in those size ranges; aggregates measured above range. Figure 5b label 167 versus text 168 for GCPP is inconsistent. No release concentration or count denominator suitable for public count summary. Do not generalize MCPP non-detection as safety or all bags as zip bags.",
@@ -318,7 +318,7 @@
     "methods": "Revised May 27, 2022. PDF pp. 2–3 §§1–2: treated drinking water, microscopic counting plus IR identification, size-fraction processing, contamination controls and laboratory-specific reporting limits.",
     "fitness": "An official analytical procedure within its treated-water scope.",
     "limitations": "Not a particle health threshold, packaging-release test, or assay for all nanoplastics. Spectroscopy on representative subsamples yields estimated confirmed counts.",
-    "findings": "<p>The procedure separates particle counting from chemical identification and requires blank controls and demonstrated laboratory performance.</p><p>The method states a lowest reliably detected particle size of {{claim:s-2f979a7f686b-lower-size}}. That is a method-scope value, not a safe exposure threshold.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>The procedure separates particle counting from chemical identification and requires blank controls and demonstrated laboratory performance.</p><p>The method states a lowest reliably detected particle size of {{claim:s-2f979a7f686b-lower-size}}. That is a method-scope value, not a safe exposure threshold.</p><p>The procedure first counts objects by microscopy and then uses infrared spectroscopy on a representative selection to establish material identity. The confirmed proportion is applied to the microscopy count to estimate microplastic abundance. This is different from chemically identifying every object originally counted. The method also places sample collection outside its scope and limits validation to its stated particle-size range. Neither a filter fraction nor an instrument name establishes capability for all smaller particles. <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/microplastics/swb-mp1-rev1.pdf\">Source: PDF pp. 2-3, sections 1.0-2.0</a>.</p><p>The document distinguishes controls according to the stage and question they address. Field blanks examine contamination associated with collection and transport; laboratory reagent blanks follow the analytical process; fortified blanks contain added particles to evaluate performance. These controls are complementary. A clean background result alone does not demonstrate recovery of particles, and successful recovery does not show that background contamination is absent. Interpreting an implemented method requires the corresponding control results and trained analytical work. <a href=\"https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/drinking_water/certlic/drinkingwater/documents/microplastics/swb-mp1-rev1.pdf\">Source: PDF pp. 3-4, section 3.0 Definitions</a>.</p>",
