     "rights": "CC BY 4.0 printed on PDF p. 1.",
     "description": "Multi-method fiber and additive characterization of commercial wet wipes purchased in the United Kingdom.",
     "scope": "Multi-method fiber and additive characterization of commercial wet wipes purchased in the United Kingdom.",
-    "findings": "<p>Conventional wipes contained polypropylene and/or polyethylene terephthalate together with cellulose. The investigated flushable and biodegradable groups were cellulose-based. Composition and organic additive signals were measured, not a release rate or infant dose.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>Conventional wipes contained polypropylene and/or polyethylene terephthalate together with cellulose. The investigated flushable and biodegradable groups were cellulose-based. Composition and organic additive signals were measured, not a release rate or infant dose.</p><p>The investigators grouped unused commercial wipes according to their package descriptions before applying complementary analytical techniques. Samples were air-dried before analysis because water could interfere with the measurements. This preparation made the material suitable for composition testing, but it also means that the analyzed specimen was not a wet wipe being used on skin or released into a sewer. The experiment addresses what the selected materials contain rather than how much they shed during use. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaap.2025.107200\">Source: PDF p. 2, section 2.1 Samples</a>.</p><p>The techniques served different purposes within that composition assessment. Electron microscopy examined fiber morphology, infrared spectroscopy examined material chemistry, and thermal methods separated signals associated with additives and fibers. Agreement between these approaches strengthens an assignment of material composition. It does not convert that assignment into a measured environmental release, an infant exposure estimate or a biodegradation result. The authors describe extension of the protocol to degradation and environmental samples as further work. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jaap.2025.107200\">Source: PDF pp. 1-2, Abstract and analytical-protocol description</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "SEM characterized fiber morphology, ATR-FTIR assessed bulk composition, and evolved-gas MS plus multi-shot pyrolysis GC-MS separated additive and fiber signatures. Air-dried, previously unused wipe material was analyzed. No particle-release experiment or release-procedure blank series was reported because the endpoint was material composition.",
     "fitness": "Strong direct evidence for why wipe fibers and chemical additives require separate endpoints and why cellulose should be distinguished from synthetic plastics.",
     "limitations": "A small selected UK product set, not all baby wipes. No transfer-to-skin or shedding count. Drying can lose volatile additives; pyrolysis has limits for polar/high-molecular-weight additives. Failure to identify an additive does not establish absence.",
@@ -740,7 +740,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY 4.0 printed on PDF p. 1.",
     "description": "Aging and particle dynamics from cut pieces of nylon, polypropylene, and cellulose tea-bag materials in water.",
     "scope": "Aging and particle dynamics from cut pieces of nylon, polypropylene, and cellulose tea-bag materials in water.",
-    "findings": "<p>Bulk chemistry identified nylon, polypropylene, and cellulose bag materials. Particle sizes and concentrations evolved differently during soaking and subsequent aging. Cellulose-derived particles must remain separate from synthetic-plastic particles.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>Bulk chemistry identified nylon, polypropylene, and cellulose bag materials. Particle sizes and concentrations evolved differently during soaking and subsequent aging. Cellulose-derived particles must remain separate from synthetic-plastic particles.</p><p>Different measurements in this experiment describe different properties. Infrared and thermal analyses characterized the tea-bag material, while light scattering and particle tracking characterized the resulting suspensions. Electrophoretic measurements addressed particle mobility and surface behavior. The tea leaves were removed, and washed pieces of bag material were used. Keeping those preparations and endpoints separate prevents a suspended-object count from being presented as individual chemical confirmation of every particle in an ordinary cup of tea. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/polym17192700\">Source: PDF pp. 3-4, sections 2.2-2.4</a>.</p><p>The biological endpoint was a cell-culture viability assay using an intestinal epithelial model. That experiment answers a narrower question than long-term effects in people drinking tea. The conclusions discuss differences between synthetic-polymer and cellulose particles and call for further health assessment of intake. Cellulose-derived material remains distinct from synthetic plastic in interpreting these experiments. The reported laboratory observations do not provide a consumer-wide safety determination or a measured human health outcome. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/polym17192700\">Source: PDF pp. 4 and 10, section 2.4 and Conclusions</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Infrared spectroscopy, calorimetry, and thermogravimetry identified bulk materials. DLS followed hydrodynamic size; NTA measured suspended-particle concentration; laser electrophoresis measured mobility. Cut and rinsed bag squares were exposed in water under short and extended soaking/aging protocols. Distilled-water DLS control showed no scattering correlation. Experiments were replicated.",
     "fitness": "Useful mechanistic evidence that time, material, and size methodology affect observed particles, particularly below ordinary microscopy windows.",
     "limitations": "Cut squares rather than intact retail bags. Some measurements followed prolonged heating/aging, not a normal cup preparation. Bulk polymer identity does not chemically identify every NTA-counted particle. Numerical NTA table resides in a supplement not reviewed here, so no exact count is reproduced. In-vitro findings do not establish consumer safety or clinical harm.",
@@ -771,7 +771,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY 4.0 printed on PDF p. 1.",
     "description": "Sampling groundwater, treatment, bottle washing, and filled/capped mineral water at bottling facilities using reusable glass bottles.",
     "scope": "Sampling groundwater, treatment, bottle washing, and filled/capped mineral water at bottling facilities using reusable glass bottles.",
-    "findings": "<p>Particle concentrations rose after filling and capping. Chemical similarity to polyethylene cap seals and low findings in uncapped controls supported cap abrasion as a source in these facilities. A glass bottle body did not prevent plastic entry from closures.</p><p class=\"hmi-table-hint\">Scroll horizontally for methods and measurement limits.</p><div class=\"hmi-table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Source-reported measurements\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reported result</th><th>Conditions</th><th>Size context</th><th>Original locator</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>{{claim:s-6f1a18e62816-capped-mineral-water}}</td><td>Filled and capped reusable glass mineral-water bottles at sampled facilities; sample category V</td><td>≥11 µm validated imaging cutoff</td><td>PDF p. 9 §3.2; Figure 4 p. 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
+    "findings": "<p>Particle concentrations rose after filling and capping. Chemical similarity to polyethylene cap seals and low findings in uncapped controls supported cap abrasion as a source in these facilities. A glass bottle body did not prevent plastic entry from closures.</p><p class=\"hmi-table-hint\">Scroll horizontally for methods and measurement limits.</p><div class=\"hmi-table-scroll\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Source-reported measurements\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reported result</th><th>Conditions</th><th>Size context</th><th>Original locator</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>{{claim:s-6f1a18e62816-capped-mineral-water}}</td><td>Filled and capped reusable glass mineral-water bottles at sampled facilities; sample category V</td><td>≥11 µm validated imaging cutoff</td><td>PDF p. 9 §3.2; Figure 4 p. 10</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The analysis used infrared images from a selected part of each filter and a trained classification model. Classification results were manually checked, and the accepted counts were extrapolated to the full filter and normalized to the sampled volume. The training data included plastics, natural material and filter-related backgrounds. This explains why the result depends on chemical classification, selection of the measured area and the particle rules, rather than being a simple count of every visible object. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/w13060841\">Source: PDF p. 5, section 2.3 FTIR Imaging and Data Evaluation</a>.</p><p>Procedural blanks were carried through the handling steps, and detection limits were calculated for the sample types. Separate recovery experiments tested particle losses during preparation, while chemical-stability checks considered effects of the treatment reagents. These checks answer different questions about the measurement. A result below the blank-based detection limit is not evidence that every particle is absent, and recovery for the tested particles does not establish equivalent performance for all sizes and polymers. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/w13060841\">Source: PDF p. 6, sections 2.4.2-2.4.4</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "FPA-FTIR imaging and random-decision-forest classification with manual review; sampled filter areas extrapolated to full-filter totals. Polymer size-detection tests, recovery checks, and procedural blanks were reported. Sample-type limits of detection were calculated from blank means and variability. Below-limit results were termed free of MPs by authors; this synthesis uses below detection limit instead.",
     "fitness": "Direct process-tracing evidence with strong quality-control detail, specific to the sampled bottling lines and size window.",
     "limitations": "Facilities and reusable-glass systems are selected, not a representative bottled-water market survey. Findings depend on blank-based thresholds, selected filter areas, model classification, and particle recovery. No nanoplastic measurement or clinical outcome. High nonplastic counts were separated from plastic results.",
@@ -957,7 +957,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 per primary PDF p. 1. Bibliographic facts and original paraphrase only; no PDF or figures republished.",
     "description": "A study of alcoholic and nonalcoholic beverages used microscopy and Raman spectroscopy to investigate particle properties and packaging groups.",
     "scope": "Alcoholic-beverage context. Main methods do not establish that the alcoholic aggregate is a beer-specific estimate.",
-    "findings": "<p>A study of alcoholic and nonalcoholic beverages used microscopy and Raman spectroscopy to investigate particle properties and packaging groups.</p><p>No numerical product measurement from this source is accepted in this packet. Useful context for polymer confirmation, mixed beverage categories, and uncertainty in packaging source attribution. No numerical beer concentration accepted.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>A study of alcoholic and nonalcoholic beverages used microscopy and Raman spectroscopy to investigate particle properties and packaging groups.</p><p>No numerical product measurement from this source is accepted in this packet. Useful context for polymer confirmation, mixed beverage categories, and uncertainty in packaging source attribution. No numerical beer concentration accepted.</p><p>The beverage study compared selected alcoholic and nonalcoholic products sold in different packaging materials. Its preparation combined filtration and chemical treatment with staining intended to distinguish natural material from candidate plastic particles. Physical appearance was recorded separately from Raman-based chemical characterization. The spectroscopy covered a subset of the unstained particles. The resulting classifications therefore depend on both the preparation decisions and the extent of chemical identification, rather than appearance alone. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2024.125388\">Source: PDF pp. 2-3, sections 2.2-2.4</a>.</p><p>The paper also calculates intake and contamination indicators using measured concentrations together with assumed consumption, body-weight or literature-reference inputs. These derived indicators are not direct observations of individual consumption or clinical effects. They must remain separate from the analytical finding that candidate particles were recovered from a beverage. This source record does not adopt a numerical beer concentration or turn a comparison between packaging groups into proof that packaging was the sole source. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envpol.2024.125388\">Source: PDF p. 3, sections 2.5-2.6</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Wet peroxide oxidation, filtration, Rose Bengal exclusion of stained natural material, stereomicroscopy and micro-Raman. Raman characterized 70% of non-stained particles per filter. Blank experiments are reported, but a numerical blank correction result is not provided in the inspected main methods/results.",
     "fitness": "Useful context for polymer confirmation, mixed beverage categories, and uncertainty in packaging source attribution. No numerical beer concentration accepted.",
     "limitations": "Alcoholic aggregate is not relabeled as beer. Raman fluorescence and residual natural material may cause misclassification or underestimation. Natural material on plastic surfaces may be excluded by staining. Main text Table 1 swaps combined alcoholic/nonalcoholic means relative to results; no numerical concentration accepted here. Available online in 2024, volume year 2025; primary volume year used.",
@@ -1019,7 +1019,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY 4.0 per primary PDF p. 1. Cite and paraphrase; no PDF or figures republished.",
     "description": "A storage study examined honey in flexible pouches with Raman particle identification and separate GC-MS analysis of extractable chemicals.",
     "scope": "Qualitative packaging-related particle evidence from specific honey pouches under outdoor, refrigerated, and dark/dry storage.",
-    "findings": "<p>A storage study examined honey in flexible pouches with Raman particle identification and separate GC-MS analysis of extractable chemicals.</p><p>No numerical product measurement from this source is accepted in this packet. Supports polymer identification and an association with pouch packaging in this experiment. Counts are not adopted because filter-area selection, extrapolation, contamination runs, and batch variation require resolution.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>A storage study examined honey in flexible pouches with Raman particle identification and separate GC-MS analysis of extractable chemicals.</p><p>No numerical product measurement from this source is accepted in this packet. Supports polymer identification and an association with pouch packaging in this experiment. Counts are not adopted because filter-area selection, extrapolation, contamination runs, and batch variation require resolution.</p><p>The honey investigation illustrates why reagent controls belong alongside a packaging comparison. The authors found a matching polymer signal associated with the container of an initially used reagent and changed the reagent supply for later experiments. Sample processing also required warming, dilution, digestion, density separation and filtration. Material observed at the end of this workflow has to be interpreted with those handling steps and contamination checks, rather than attributed automatically to the original pouch. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/microplastics4040090\">Source: PDF pp. 4-5, sections 2.2-2.3</a>.</p><p>Raman measurements were compared with the inside and outside packaging materials, while infrared measurements characterized the packaging itself. A separate extraction and chromatography workflow investigated chemical migrants in honey. These are distinct analytical endpoints: identification of a dissolved or extractable compound does not count plastic particles. This source record preserves those distinctions and the preparation limits. It does not adopt the paper's extrapolated particle totals or convert them into an intake or mass estimate. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/microplastics4040090\">Source: PDF pp. 5-6, sections 2.3-2.6</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Honey dilution, warming, peroxide digestion, sodium bromide separation and sequential filtration. Material larger than 74 µm discarded, then 1.2 µm final filters examined by Raman microscopy. Spectra compared against inner PE and outer PET packaging, with ATR-FTIR bulk-film characterization. Some early runs contaminated by peroxide storage container; replacement reagent described. Selected filter areas with differing particle density used for extrapolation, excluding center/edges. GC-MS measures extractable chemicals separately.",
     "fitness": "Supports polymer identification and an association with pouch packaging in this experiment. Counts are not adopted because filter-area selection, extrapolation, contamination runs, and batch variation require resolution.",
     "limitations": "No accepted numeric pouch-release rate or mass estimate. Table 1 has contaminated runs and observed versus statistical counts; field selection is not uniform whole-filter enumeration. Greater-than-sieve aggregates occur despite prefiltration; pore size is not validated detection limit. Storage-control and outdoor trends are not universally monotonic. Crystallized honey and sample recovery differed. Cannot extend to all honey packages or treat GC-MS chemicals as particle counts.",
@@ -1205,7 +1205,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 per primary PDF p. 23. Bibliographic facts and original paraphrase only; no PDF or figures republished.",
     "description": "A narrative review discusses infant exposure pathways and summarizes studies of milk, formula, water, produce, and other foods.",
     "scope": "Context-only review; no original test of packaged infant puree in the supplied category folder.",
-    "findings": "<p>A narrative review discusses infant exposure pathways and summarizes studies of milk, formula, water, produce, and other foods.</p><p>No numerical product measurement from this source is accepted in this packet. Useful for mapping questions and leads to original studies. Not accepted as a source of a numerical infant-puree concentration or product-specific health claim.</p>",
+    "findings": "<p>A narrative review discusses infant exposure pathways and summarizes studies of milk, formula, water, produce, and other foods.</p><p>No numerical product measurement from this source is accepted in this packet. Useful for mapping questions and leads to original studies. Not accepted as a source of a numerical infant-puree concentration or product-specific health claim.</p><p>This article brings together exposure pathways and findings reported in other publications. Its discussion of infant foods moves between breast milk, water, formula and other dietary sources, with references attached to the underlying studies. Those categories cannot be collapsed into a measurement of packaged infant puree. The review can help identify questions and original papers for later assessment, while the sampling conditions and analytical results remain the responsibility of each cited investigation. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s44169-025-00099-z\">Source: PDF pp. 1 and 9, review identity, Abstract and infant-food discussion</a>.</p><p>The future-research section identifies analytical limitations and a need for consistent methods and reporting units. It also states that much of the toxicological evidence comes from animal models and that applying those findings to human health has substantial limitations. That distinction matters when reading the broader discussion of infant vulnerability. This source is a synthesis of concerns and research gaps, rather than a new clinical experiment or an independently measured product concentration. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/s44169-025-00099-z\">Source: PDF p. 22, Perspectives for Future Research</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Literature synthesis, not a new consumer-product experiment. Its food tables attribute measurements to other papers. No original puree sampling, particle extraction, polymer confirmation, recovery, or blank-control data.",
     "fitness": "Useful for mapping questions and leads to original studies. Not accepted as a source of a numerical infant-puree concentration or product-specific health claim.",
     "limitations": "Only file in infant-food-puree folder. Broad infant-food discussion includes milk, formula, water, produce, meat and flour, but none becomes a packaged-puree test merely because an infant may eat it. Tables and toxicology results are secondary, not original findings. Original source retrieval/review needed before numerical acceptance.",
@@ -1356,7 +1356,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY-NC-ND per PDF p. 1. Cite and paraphrase; no PDF or figures redistributed.",
     "description": "A household dust survey chemically identified selected particles in sieved settled-dust fractions, separating synthetic polymers from cellulose.",
     "scope": "Floor dust from single-person residences in Japan; composition survey, not measured air concentration.",
-    "findings": "<p>A household dust survey chemically identified selected particles in sieved settled-dust fractions, separating synthetic polymers from cellulose.</p><ul><li>{{claim:s-b77f33e84d3c-dustsamples}}; Single-person residences in four Japanese regions; collected using vacuum cleaners.</li><li>{{claim:s-b77f33e84d3c-petshare}}; Selected dust particles, not total dust mass or all airborne fibers.</li></ul>",
+    "findings": "<p>A household dust survey chemically identified selected particles in sieved settled-dust fractions, separating synthetic polymers from cellulose.</p><ul><li>{{claim:s-b77f33e84d3c-dustsamples}}; Single-person residences in four Japanese regions; collected using vacuum cleaners.</li><li>{{claim:s-b77f33e84d3c-petshare}}; Selected dust particles, not total dust mass or all airborne fibers.</li></ul><p>Participants collected settled floor dust using household vacuum cleaners, and a questionnaire recorded features of their living spaces. That design links the recovered material to the sampled residences and collection procedure. It is not an air sampler measuring what a resident breathed. Differences between building or furnishing categories in this observational survey also do not isolate a single source, because the recorded household features were not controlled experimental exposures. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/2475-8876.70013\">Source: PDF p. 2, sections 2.1-2.2</a>.</p><p>The chemical analysis used particles selected from sieved size fractions, while optical and electron microscopy described morphology. Composition among the selected particles is a different quantity from the total amount of dust, an airborne concentration or an inhaled dose. Cellulose and synthetic polymers must also remain distinct when interpreting the material categories. The source supports a description of the selected settled-dust samples, with the sampling and identification limits retained rather than extended to all indoor exposure. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/2475-8876.70013\">Source: PDF pp. 2-3, sections 2.3-2.4</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Participants vacuum-collected settled dust; sieved fractions underwent random particle selection and KBr-plate microFTIR identification, with optical and SEM morphology. Clean laboratory handling described, but no quantified procedural blank result located.",
     "fitness": "Supports presence and composition of polymers in the selected settled-dust particles.",
     "limitations": "Selected particles do not establish total particles per gram or air volume. Cellulose was abundant and is not automatically plastic. No quantified blank result located. Size/region summary percentages vary across the paper, so no aggregate polymer percentage is used. Publication is the 2025 volume despite the acquisition filename and copyright dating to 2024.",
@@ -1386,7 +1386,7 @@
     "rights": "CC BY 4.0 per PDF p. 1. Paraphrase only.",
     "description": "A clinical pilot screened placenta and meconium while examining operating-room materials, airborne fallout and negative controls.",
     "scope": "Clinical human sample screening; methodological pilot.",
-    "findings": "<p>A clinical pilot screened placenta and meconium while examining operating-room materials, airborne fallout and negative controls.</p><ul><li>{{claim:s-d2f3af1338e5-size-window}}; FTIR clinical pilot; placenta and meconium tables.</li></ul>",
+    "findings": "<p>A clinical pilot screened placenta and meconium while examining operating-room materials, airborne fallout and negative controls.</p><ul><li>{{claim:s-d2f3af1338e5-size-window}}; FTIR clinical pilot; placenta and meconium tables.</li></ul><p>The clinical pilot changed its sampling procedure after examining the initial results. The later phase included washed placental tissue, tissue taken from the placental core and a collection of airborne fallout in the operating room. Materials used in delivery and preparation were also investigated. These comparisons are central to the study question: a signal in a clinical specimen must be considered alongside opportunities for contamination during collection and handling. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics13070921\">Source: PDF p. 3, sections 2.1-2.2 Study Population and Sample Collecting</a>.</p><p>The discussion shows why the controls affect source attribution. Polymer findings in clinical materials and airborne fallout complicated the interpretation of signals in biological samples. A polymer that appeared informative in the initial phase could also be found in a possible contamination source examined later. The paper therefore supports attention to sampling and contamination control; this pilot does not establish population prevalence, a route from a particular consumer product or a demonstrated clinical consequence. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/pharmaceutics13070921\">Source: PDF pp. 8-9, results and Discussion</a>.</p>",
     "methods": "Chemical digestion, coarse sieving and FTIR microscopy screened a specified polymer panel. Sampling, instrument/container negative controls, procedural blank, clinical material checks and operating-room fallout were assessed.",
     "fitness": "Demonstrates why human tissue findings require detailed contamination controls.",
     "limitations": "Very small pilot; several negative controls were positive. Some sample and control polymers overlap, making counts or general prevalence unsuitable. Clinical handling complicates attribution. Positive screening does not establish fetal injury or a route from a named product.",
